ments in this regard. Warwick et al.4 and Rajendran et al.5 highlight the importance of eVTOL technology for developing air taxi service for UAM and also provided a critical review of these developments. Manufacturers including Uber,6 Rolls Royce,7 Airbus and Volocopter GmbH have unveiled various prototypes or conceptual designs for UAVs. Joshi8 worked on flapping type wing miniature UAVs with different shapes of the wings and various flow conditions utilizing CFD tools for the analysis purpose and highlighted one additional merit of UAVs, being small in size which allows them to move in confined spaces. Houghton and Hoburg9 conducted early research on monocopter UAV operations in confined spaces and indoor operations employing CFD analysis, parametric modelling and flight tests. Their study mentions that similar to traditional helicopters, these small UAVs also generate thrust by a constant rotational motion of the rotor blades with great capability of indoor and outdoor operations in confined spaces. The unique ability of vertical takeoff and landing has attracted many engineers and researchers. Otsuka and Nagatani10 presented an overlapping configuration of rotors to reduce the thrust loss due to rotor-wake interactions in hover and also highlighted the fact that these small UAVs are able to perform several tasks by just controlling the rotational speeds of their rotors. Electric Vertical Takeoff and Landing (eVTOL) vehicles are able to operate at much higher speeds than the corresponding traditional helicopters with a main rotor and a tail rotor having limitations of blade tip velocity. This advantage of eVTOL vehicles is also seen in research of Nugroho et al.11 and Chahba et al.,12 who performed analysis at different high RPMs for rotors of such multirotor which is not feasible for helicopter rotors. At present, in addition to some customized configurations with different rotor arrangements, tricopters, quadcopters, hexacopters and octocopters are commonly available in market.13–16 Different configurations have different thrusting capability, operation time and range depending on the type of given task.17–19 Although multirotor UAVs have their promising applications and significant benefits, there are a few challenges associated as well which require technical improvements to enhance their aerodynamic performance. Ko and Lee20 worked on the rotor-wake interactions of multirotor in forward flight and Lakshminarayan and Baeder21 performed analysis of rotor wake interaction of coaxial rotors for multirotor in hover and both researches conclude that unlike traditional helicopters, the rotors of small multirotor UAVs are significantly affected by the trailed wake system both in hover and forward flight since each rotor remains in the proximity of such wake, especially in hovering state. Currently available small multirotor UAVs have operating time of less than an hour, which indicates a need of improvements to enhance this factor. One effective way to achieve this goal is to enhance aerodynamic efficiency of rotors by reducing interaction with neighboring rotors. Aeroacoustics noise is another phenomena besides aerodynamic performance which is also linked comprehensively with rotor-to-rotor interaction in small multirotor UAVs. Different multirotor configurations have different extent of rotor-wake interaction and developing new configurations which can minimize such interactions is a must. Therefore, an accurate prediction of rotor-to-rotor interaction is quite essential for aerodynamically efficient design of small multirotor UAVs.
Even though various studies are being conducted on small multirotor UAVs, very limited literature is available for rotor-to-rotor interaction and its influence on aerodynamic efficiency of rotor in different small multirotor UAV configurations especially in forward flight. It might be because of the fact that rotor-to-rotor interaction is more evident in hover than in forward flight case. Except for Yoon et al.22 who performed computational analysis on aerodynamic performance of multirotor in a quadcopter configuration and concluded that vertical forces of the quadcopter are significantly influenced by rotor interaction in hovering state. Many scholars23–26 conducted analysis on aerodynamic performance of coaxial rotor configuration in different flight conditions employing CFD methods, since coaxial rotors in hovering state have the most severe wake interaction. Russell et al.27 carried out a comprehensive study through quantifying thrusts of five multirotor UAVs at different vehicle attitudes, rotor rotational speeds and wind speeds. Research is also focused on developing new tools for accurate and fast results prediction for multirotor studies involving very heavy grids. Tugnoli et al.28 developed a tool called DUST which is an open source code developed with MIT license for the aforementioned purpose, Caprace et al.29 studied the quadcopter in forward flight with focus on the influence on aerodynamic performance of multirotor due to interaction of airframe of the vehicle with the wake of rotors using vortex particle mesh method which is another CFD based method used widely for wake studies and Zhang et al. 30 studied high fidelity aerodynamic and aeroacoustics modeling processes which support preliminary design of large eVTOL vehicles, employing CFD tools. They also focused on aerodynamic interference effects between rotors and other components. Yang et al.31 performed simulations and experimental analysis on an isolated multirotor propeller in forward flight at different advance ratios to understand thrust and torque characteristics. In present research, rotor-to-rotor interaction in octocopters is studied employing CFD tools to understand the influence of such interaction on aerodynamic performance of the octocopters in conventional and coaxial configurations. Thrust, torque as well as figure of merit for each configuration is provided to understand the aerodynamic behavior of each configuration. The mathematical representation of the combined figure of merit for eight rotors of the different octocopter configurations is given in the study of Shukla and Komerath32 and mentioned in Eq. (1).
