2.1. Overset grid method
Overset grid method is considered as one of the most widely used meshing methods for rotor analysis33–35 due to its great accuracy for steady and unsteady low analysis. An overset grid model comprises a fitted grid near a body, placed above a simpler underlying mesh, having the region of underlying mesh blanked out. Fig. 1 shows a typical overset grid model. Interface conditions are employed at the overset mesh boundaries in order to couple the solution between them. A drawback associated with this method is the involved computational cost which makes it not preferable for multirotor studies.
2.2. Virtual blade method
Virtual Blade Method (VBM) is a typical implementation of Blade Element Momentum theory (BEM) developed by Zori and Rajagopalan36 for helicopter rotors application. It does not require actual rotor blade geometry or blade mesh, but simulates rotating blades effect on fluid through a body force acting inside a disk of fluid having an area equal to the swept area of actual rotor disk. Body force value of time averaged
over a cycle from blade element method37 calculated forces. It requires tables of lift coefficient and drag coefficient of selected airfoil at operating Mach numbers and angle of attacks along with geometric properties of the blades to replicate the flow effects of actual blades. In BEM, a rotor blade is composed of several small segments or control points from root to tip of the blade. The drag and lift forces on each section are computed from 2D based on chord length, lift and drag coefficient of each segment, angle of attack, and airfoil type. Inlet boundary freestream velocity serves as the initial value for calculating angle of attack of each control point along the blade as shown in Eq. (3).
