2.4. Validation of numerical method
For the verification of selected VBM method, simulations were conducted and compared with available experimental data from studies of Zhou et al.38 to confirm the accuracy of selected method for current analysis. DJI Phantom 2 rotor models are used herein for the abovementioned purpose. The geometric and flow characteristics of the model are given in Fig. 5 and Table 2. The comparison between experimental results and simulation results employing VBM in cases of a single isolated rotor as well as two side by side rotors experiencing rotor-to-rotor interaction is demonstrated in terms of the flow ensembleaverage velocity at a plane located 0.1D downstream of the rotors in Fig. 6 and Fig. 7. The color map in the Fig. 6 represents axial velocity while the arrows show swirl velocity. The rotor tip speed is 61 m/s. Simulation result shows a bit of difference especially at the hub region. Due to this reason, the isolated rotor simulation converges to mean thrust coefficient value of 0.1005 and steady fluctuation value of 0.0011, while the experimental studies of Zhou et al.38 and Ning 39 mention the experimental mean thrust coefficient of 0.1013 and steady fluctuation of 0.008. The error in result of mean thrust coefficient is about 0.5%, whereas the fluctuations are under predicted in the simulations. It can be assumed that the mounting pole/test stand had significant interactions resulting in fluctuations with the rotor which is not simulated in current VBM simulation. It can be observed that the wake structure is circular in isolated rotor case obtained through experiment as well as VBM simulation but it becomes conic in the region
having two rotor tip in proximity of each other showing the interference effects on the shape of the wake. Since the two rotors are rotating in opposite direction, an upwash is generated between the rotors when the advancing rotor tips of two rotors come at the same location in proximity of each other. This phenomenon can be observed from both experiment and VBM simulation. Fig. 7 demonstrates ensemble-averaged velocity as flow converted downstream of the single rotor and twin rotor cases for experimental and VBM simulation results. The results from experiments and VBM simulations are quite similar. From both studies, radial contraction of the induced flow (W component of velocity) is found showing that the high velocity region at rotor tip location (X/D = 0.4) converge to X/D = 0.3 near the root region as the flow moves downstream. The distribution of velocity is similar in both studies. Compared to isolated rotor case, the velocity field of twin rotor case in Fig. 7 is slightly dragged towards the neighboring rotor which could be the result of Coanda effect (i.e. phenomenon describing the flow to be attracted towards the nearby object). Due to this effect, the velocity in twin rotor case is observed to be lower than isolated rotor case in X/D = 0.5 to X/D = 0.2 region while the velocity distribution is almost the same on both sides of the isolated rotor.
