3.2.2. Effect of vertical spacing in coaxial octocopters on rotorto-rotor interaction
Vertical spacing (S) between the upper and lower layers of rotors could be a crucial factor in design of these configurations, therefore, a comparative study is carried out to study
the effect of variation in vertical spacing on rotor-to-rotor interaction and subsequently on thrust and torque magnitude of the vehicle. Hybrid configuration showed no significant influence of vertical spacing on thrust and torque magnitude as far as the spacing it not reduced to a level that it starts behaving like a conventional configuration. Coaxial configuration is studied for vertical spacing of 0.125R, 0.5R and 1.5R between the two rotor layers. The velocity isosurface, ensemble-average velocity and distribution of sectional lift along the azimuth of rotor are demonstrated in Fig. 13, Fig. 14 and Fig. 15. Thrust distribution is illustrated only for rotors 1, 2, 3 because similar characteristics are shown by the rest of the rotors also. It is observed that the degradation of thrust of lower rotor layer in hover is at the central region of the rotors, since the upper rotor wake contracts and goes into the central region of lower rotor and tip region is not effected much. This effect is quite significant from rotor root to 0.8R span. Fig. 14 shows the ensemble-average velocity 0.1D below the rotor layer for each vertical spacing case. Comparing upper layers show only 0.125R effected at locations where it comes in proximity of lower rotor blades. The lower layers show increase in downwash at the center region with increase in vertical spacing leading to lower thrust. Similar results are found in the studies of Lakshminarayan 40 and Park41 et al. From Fig. 13, it is observed that very less wake interaction in coaxial rotors having vertical spacing of 0.125R and wake structure is not destroyed much for this case. This shows that at very less vertical distance, wake interaction is not significant in coaxial rotors. In fact, at very small vertical spacing, wake interference is dominated by loading effect. When two coaxial rotors are very close, they effect pressure distribution on the surfaces facing each other. The lower surface of upper layer of rotors enter low pressure region due to low pressure on upper surface of lower layer of rotors leading to reduction of pressure gradient on surfaces of upper layer of rotors. Similarly, the upper surface of lower layer of rotors enter high pressure region due to high pressure on lower surface of upper layer of rotors leading to increase in pressure of upper surface of lower layer of rotor. This subsequently decrease pressure gradient on the surfaces of lower layer of rotors. Reduction in pressure gradient on both upper and lower layer rotors takes place leading to reduction in thrust of both upper layer of rotors and lower layer of rotors. Fig. 16 shows increase in pressure of upper surface of lower
