3.3. Forward flight analysis
Two types of forward flight is considered herein i.e. quasisteady forward flight and steady forward flight. Since transition from hover to forward flight of an octocopter is achieved
by reduction in rpm of fore rotors and increase in rpm of aft rotors so that the vehicle can tilt forward, this type of motion with different rotation speeds of fore rotors and aft rotors with approximation of insignificant difference in thrust on different rotors is called as quasi-steady forward flight. After achieving the desired tilt angle, the octocopter needs to increase the rpm of fore rotors and reduce rpm of aft rotors in order to achieve same rpm on all rotors so that it does not tilt further and it can move forward horizontally. This type of forward flight has same rpm on all rotors of an octocopter. It is called as steady forward flight. Although very less literature in available on forward flight of a multirotor, most of it considers steady forward flight case. All forward flight simulations for present study are carried out at angle of attack of 7.7°. The distance between two layers of rotors in each configuration is kept 0.5R. Radius and geometry of rotors is same as used in hover analysis. Fig. 22 shows the phenomenon of transition from hover to forward flight and steady forward flight. Simulations are performed first on an isolated rotor under same flow conditions as those which will be employed on dif
